    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Flow equations",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 5
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The conservation of momentum in the flow is described by the *Navier-Stokes (N-S) Equation:*",
        "page_idx": 5
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "**F** are accelerations due to volumetric forces:",
        "page_idx": 5
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "is the pressure, and *μ* is the viscosity of the fluid.",
        "page_idx": 5
    },
    {
        "type": "list",
        "list_items": [
            "- Unknowns: <strong>v</strong> and <em>p</em>",
            "- N-S + Continuity eq. form a system of Nonlinear PDE",
            "- No analytical solution exists for realistic ship flows.",
            "- Numerical solutions are still far from feasible",
            "- Practical approaches: RANS (CFD)"
        ],
        "page_idx": 5
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 5
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 5
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 5
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 5
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Potential theory",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 6
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "A further simplification is obtained by assuming that the fluid is *inviscid* and the flow is *irrotational.* Irrotaional means that",
        "page_idx": 6
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Under this assumption, then exists a scalar function     called *potential* such that",
        "page_idx": 6
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "So, if we know the potential, we can calculate the flow velocity vector (the gradient of the potential).",
        "page_idx": 6
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 6
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 6
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 6
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 6
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "How do we obtain the potential?",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 7
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "In potential theory,  the continuity equation reverts to the *Laplacian* of the potential equal to zero:",
        "page_idx": 7
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The potential is, thus, obtained by solving this  subject to appropriate boundary conditions, *i.e.,* by solving a boundary value problem (VBP).",
        "page_idx": 7
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The Laplace Equation is linear ⇔ Superposition of flows.",
        "page_idx": 7
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 7
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 7
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 7
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 7
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "How do we calculate pressure?",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 8
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "If we neglect viscosity in the N-S equation, we obtain the Euler Equation of flow:",
        "page_idx": 8
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Then,",
        "page_idx": 8
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "where",
        "page_idx": 8
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 8
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 8
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 8
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 8
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Irrotaional flow assumption",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 9
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Using some vector calculus",
        "page_idx": 9
    },
    {
        "type": "image",
        "img_path": "images/48093a7de6428870328d6535e073c15ffb2e5f049a9237b7de55c69cba1c171d.jpg",
        "image_caption": [],
        "page_idx": 9
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "If the flow is irrotational, then",
        "page_idx": 9
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "where",
        "page_idx": 9
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 9
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 9
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 9
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 9
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Bernoulli equation",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 10
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "If this is valid in the whole fluid, then",
        "page_idx": 10
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "which is the Bernoulli equation.",
        "page_idx": 10
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 10
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 10
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 10
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 10
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Potential theory—summary",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Potential theory offers a great simplification: if we know the potential, then we know the velocity and the pressure, from which we can calculate the forces acting on a floating body by integrating the pressure over the surface of the body.",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<em>Inviscid</em> <em>fluid</em> and <em>irrotational flow</em>",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "*Potential*",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "*Flow velocity*",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "*Pressure*",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "for *most* problems related ship motion in waves, potential theory is sufficient for engineering purposes. Viscous effects are added to the models using empirical formulae or via system identification.",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 11
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Applications",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 12
    },
    {
        "type": "list",
        "list_items": [
            "- Regular waves",
            "- Marine structures in waves"
        ],
        "page_idx": 12
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 12
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 12
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 12
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 12
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Regular waves in deep water",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 13
    },
    {
        "type": "image",
        "img_path": "images/96b0f6dc7a7010b86eca777a8283e801bc10ced57a43f1a405385c7d4fc50e38.jpg",
        "image_caption": [],
        "page_idx": 13
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 13
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 13
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 13
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 13
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Kinematic free-surface Condition",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Kinematic free-surface condition:  A fluid particle on the free surface is assumed to remain on the free surface.",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Let the free surface be defined as",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$z=\\zeta (x,y,t)$",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Then, if",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$F:=z-\\zeta (x,y,t)$",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$\\frac{DF}{Dt}=0$",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The kinematic condition reverts to",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Hence,",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$\\frac{\\partial \\zeta }{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial \\zeta }{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial \\zeta }{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial z}=0$",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "on",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$z=\\zeta (x,y,t)$",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 14
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Dynamic free-surface Conditions",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Dynamic free-surface condition:  the water pressure equals the atmospheric pressure on the free surface.",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "If we choose the constant in the Bernoulli equation as",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$C={p_{0}}/{\\rho }$",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Then,",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$g\\zeta +\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial t}+\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial x}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial y}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial z}\\right)^{2}\\right]=0$",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "on",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$z=\\zeta (x,y,t)$",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 15
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Linearised free-surface conditions",
        "text_level": 2,
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "The free-surface conditions can be linearised about the mean free-surface:",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$\\frac{\\partial \\zeta }{\\partial t}-\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial z}=0$",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$g\\zeta +\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial t}=0$",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "on",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$z=0$",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Combined:",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$g\\frac{\\partial \\varphi }{\\partial z}+\\frac{\\partial ^{2}\\varphi }{\\partial t^{2}}=0$",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "on",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "$z=0$",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "06/06/26",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "One-day Tutorial, CAMS'07, Bol, Croatia",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
    {
        "type": "page_footnote",
        "text": "<number>",
        "page_idx": 16
    },
